A team ports a frequent-flyer perks calculator from Java to Go, aiming for a direct line-by-line translation. The Java original:

```java
Set<String> perksForTier(String tier) {
    Set<String> perks = new HashSet<>();
    switch (tier) {
        case "GOLD":
            perks.add("lounge_access");
            // intentional: higher tiers include every lower tier's perks
        case "SILVER":
            perks.add("priority_boarding");
        case "BRONZE":
            perks.add("free_checked_bag");
            break;
        default:
            // no perks
    }
    return perks;
}
```

The Go port, already reviewed and merged:

```go
func perksForTier(tier string) []string {
    var perks []string
    switch tier {
    case "GOLD":
        perks = append(perks, "lounge_access")
        break
    case "SILVER":
        perks = append(perks, "priority_boarding")
        break
    case "BRONZE":
        perks = append(perks, "free_checked_bag")
        break
    default:
        // no perks
    }
    return perks
}
```

The PR description says: 'Direct port -- same case order and same break placement as the Java version, so behavior is unchanged.' The reviewer agreed and approved it; the only existing test calls `perksForTier("BRONZE")` and checks for one perk. Is the PR description correct? If not, what does a GOLD-tier account actually receive from each version, and what should change?

