A forum server-renders comment threads to HTML. A stored-XSS report: a comment whose body was `<img src=x onerror=alert(1)>` executed when the thread page loaded, because the body was written into the page markup without escaping.

The thread is rendered by `renderThread.ts`:

```ts
// renderThread.ts -- renders a comment and its replies to an HTML string
import { escapeHtml } from './escape';

type Comment = { author: string; body: string; replies: Comment[] };

function renderReply(reply: Comment): string {
  return `
    <li class="reply">
      <span class="who">${escapeHtml(reply.author)}</span>
      <div class="reply-body">${reply.body}</div>
      <ul>${reply.replies.map(renderReply).join('')}</ul>
    </li>`;
}

export function renderThread(comment: Comment): string {
  return `
    <article class="comment">
      <span class="who">${escapeHtml(comment.author)}</span>
      <div class="body">${escapeHtml(comment.body)}</div>
      <ul>${comment.replies.map(renderReply).join('')}</ul>
    </article>`;
}
```

(The `escapeHtml(comment.body)` call on the top-level body is the fix; before it, that line interpolated `comment.body` directly.)

The same comment bodies are also rendered into the daily notification email by `renderDigest.ts`, which already escapes them:

```ts
// renderDigest.ts -- renders the day's top-level comments into an email table
import { escapeHtml } from './escape';

export function renderDigest(comments: Comment[]): string {
  return comments
    .map(c => `<tr><td>${escapeHtml(c.author)}</td><td>${escapeHtml(c.body)}</td></tr>`)
    .join('');
}
```

The PR author's note: "renderDigest.ts renders the same comment bodies and already runs them through escapeHtml; this brings renderThread.ts in line with it." A regression test posts a top-level comment containing `<script>` and asserts the rendered thread contains the escaped form -- it passes. The reviewer diffs the two files, confirms both now pass every comment body through escapeHtml, and approves the fix as complete. Is the approval sound?

An internal analytics dashboard shows a few headline numbers -- active accounts, total balance, open tickets. Computing them live would scan several large tables on every page load, so a scheduled job precomputes them once a night and writes the results into a small summary table:

```sql
CREATE TABLE account_summary (
  metric      text        NOT NULL,
  value       bigint      NOT NULL,
  computed_at timestamptz NOT NULL DEFAULT now()
);
```

```python
# jobs/recompute_summary.py -- run by cron at 02:00 every night
def recompute():
    rows = [
        ("active_accounts", count_active_accounts()),
        ("total_balance",   sum_balances()),
        ("open_tickets",    count_open_tickets()),
    ]
    for metric, value in rows:
        db.execute(
            "INSERT INTO account_summary (metric, value) VALUES (%s, %s)",
            (metric, value),
        )

if __name__ == "__main__":
    try:
        recompute()
    except Exception as e:
        logging.error("summary recompute failed: %s", e)
        # don't crash the box or spam the pager over a transient blip
```

